---
// Goal: show that you can put a Jev-class decision model in your code with
// ten simple functions, whatever model, language or database you use, and
// why ThinkThen beats calling the model yourself.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { CODE_FUNCTIONS, FUNCTIONS, HOWTOS, COUNTS } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { run } from '../data/samples.mjs';
import overhead from '../data/overhead.json';

const decide = FUNCTIONS.find((f) => f.name === 'decide');
const refund = { ...run('functions/decide', '0-refund'), see: decide.see['0-refund'] };
const jevMs = Math.round(overhead.head.live.median);
const sixHowtos = HOWTOS.slice(0, 6);
---
<Base title="ThinkThen">
  <h1>Code that knows what you mean.</h1>
  <p class="lede">ThinkThen is ten functions on top of your Jev model, whatever kind of model you use: on-prem, hosted, TypeSafe or another vendor. It gives you a simple way to use these models. Hand one a message, ask "Does the customer ask for a refund?", and get back yes or no.</p>

  <Terminal run={refund} caption={refund.see} />
  <p>The same ten functions run in Python, TypeScript, SQL and the rest of the <a href="/install/">{COUNTS.bindings}</a>.</p>

  <p><code>decide</code> is an if that understands. <code>filter</code> is a grep that understands. <code>rank</code> is a sort that understands. Every answer rests on the model's probabilities. On <code>decide</code>, a band sends anything between its two sides back as not sure. A person reads those.</p>

  <h2>The ten functions</h2>
  <p class="counts">{COUNTS.functions}, <a href="/install/shell/">{COUNTS.cli}</a>, <a href="/install/">{COUNTS.bindings}</a>.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Function</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    {CODE_FUNCTIONS.map((fn) => (
      <tr><td><a href={`/functions/${fn.name}/`}><code>{fn.name}</code></a></td><td>{fn.line}</td></tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/functions/question-file/">Save a question as a file</a></p>

  <h2>Why not call the model yourself?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You write the question in plain words. Jev needs no labelled data ahead of time. <a href="/functions/decide/">decide</a> shows one question and its answer.</li>
    <li>Jev is fast and cheap. On 2026-10-01 a call to Jev took a median of {jevMs} ms, and ThinkThen's own work took about 2 ms of it. <a href="/learn/overhead/">Overhead</a> gives every measurement and the published price.</li>
    <li>ThinkThen batches for you. Hand it a list, a column or a stream, and it packs the records into as few requests as the batch settings allow. See <a href="/learn/functional/">Functional patterns</a>.</li>
    <li>ThinkThen caches and replays. A repeated question reuses its saved answer. A test replays recorded answers with no key and no network. See <a href="/learn/caching/">Caching and replay</a>.</li>
    <li>You can swap the model and the provider. One <code>--backend</code> option points the same call at TypeSafe, another hosted vendor, or a model on your own machine. See <a href="/install/backends/">Backends</a>.</li>
    <li>ThinkThen is yours. It runs from the command line and every binding, databases included. No single vendor's SQL function holds your logic. See <a href="/install/">Install</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Jobs your software can take on</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {sixHowtos.map((h) => (
      <a class="tile" href={`/how-tos/${h.slug}/`}>
        <b>{h.title}</b>
        <span>{h.reader}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/how-tos/">See every how-to</a></p>

  <h2>Learn it on the Beatles</h2>
  <p><a href="/learn/beatles-bench/">Beatles Bench</a> asks questions about Beatles songs. A script set the right answer to each scored question ahead of time, from Wikipedia and Wikidata. New to ThinkThen? Start with the <a href="/learn/tutorial/">tutorial</a>.</p>
</Base>
